ce:section.ce:para:
The pilot-scale experiment was conducted in a production workshop in Heilongjiang Province, northeast China (longitude 125°22′–126°22′ and latitude 45°10′–46°20′). The wastewater was collected from a pilot-scale production line that produces ethanol using corn stover (Fig. 1
                        ). The corn stover was pretreated using a steam-explosion device, and a frame filter was used to obtain filtrate from the fermenting mash. The fermenting mash from the production line contained a large quantity of SS, and the frame filter was used to reduce the SS content of the wastewater used in this study. The characteristics of the filtered wastewater are summarized in Table 1
                        . The wastewater was stored in a large tank before its use in the bioreactors.

Each bioreactor was started individually, and the raw wastewater was diluted in the initial phase. The start-up of the CSTR was divided into three stages before connection with the IC, and start-up of the IC was divided into two stages before the connection (Fig. 3
                           ). The CSTR was started using diluted raw wastewater for sludge acclimation, and the input COD was about 2000mg/L (stage I, 13d), 3500mg/L (stage II, 16d) and 5000mg/L (stage III, 7d). The IC also was started using diluted raw wastewater, and the input COD was increased from approximately 1300mg/L (stage I, 11d) to 1800mg/L (stage II, 24d). When the COD removal efficiency of both the CSTR and IC reached a steady state (approximately 70%), the two bioreactors were connected. After the connection, the input COD of the CSTR was gradually increased from approximately 5000mg/L (at 36d) to 10,500mg/L (at 65d) (Fig. 4
                           ).
